/**
 * <p>Checks whether the given String is a valid Java number.</p>
 *
 * <p>A valid number can be:</p>
 * <ul>
 *     <li>An optional leading sign ('+' or '-').</li>
 *     <li>A hexadecimal number, prefixed with "0x" or "0X" (e.g., "0x1a", "-0xAF").</li>
 *     <li>An integer (e.g., "123", "-456", "+789").</li>
 *     <li>A decimal number (e.g., "1.23", "-0.5", "+0.5").</li>
 *     <li>A number in scientific notation (e.g., "1.2e-3", "1E+5").</li>
 *     <li>A number with a type qualifier (e.g., "123L", "1.2f", "1.2D").</li>
 * </ul>
 *
 * <p>This method is designed to be consistent with the parsing behavior of
 * {@link org.apache.commons.lang3.math.NumberUtils#createNumber(String)}.
 * Therefore, it returns {@code true} for strings that {@code createNumber} can
 * successfully parse into a {@link java.lang.Number} object, and {@code false} otherwise.</p>
 *
 * <p>Specifically, the following are NOT considered valid numbers by this method:</p>
 * <ul>
 *     <li>Null or empty strings.</li>
 *     <li>Strings consisting only of a sign (e.g., "+", "-").</li>
 *     <li>Strings like "0x" or "-0x" (hexadecimal prefix without digits).</li>
 *     <li>Strings starting with a decimal point but no leading digit (e.g., ".5", "+.5", "-.5").</li>
 *     <li>Strings ending with a decimal point (e.g., "1.").</li>
 *     <li>Strings ending with an exponent character (e.g., "1E", "1E+", "1E-").</li>
 *     <li>Long type qualifiers ('l' or 'L') used with decimal points or exponents (e.g., "1.2L", "1EL").</li>
 * </ul>
